= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Taylor series> $\sin x=x-x^3/6+O(x^5)$ gives $\sin^2x=x^2(1-x^2/3+O(x^4))$. Taking its reciprocal and cancelling the singular term gives
$$
\frac1{\sin^2x}-\frac1{x^2}=\frac1{x^2}\left[1+\frac{x^2}{3}+O(x^4)-1\right]=\boxed{\frac13+O(x^2)}.
$$
Thus the apparent singularity at zero is removable, and the first nonzero term is the constant $1/3$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Taylor series> of the <sine> and <arctangent> give $\sin x-\arctan x=x^3/6+O(x^5)$, while $x^2\ln(1+x)=x^3(1-x/2+O(x^2))$. Dividing the two series gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\sin x-\arctan x}{x^2\ln(1+x)}=\frac16+O(x).}
$$
The first nonzero term after cancellation is the constant $1/6$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Factor the numerator as $(e^x-1)^2=x^2+O(x^3)$. The <Taylor series> of the <cosine> gives $\cos3x-2\cos2x+\cos x=(-9/2+4-1/2)x^2+O(x^4)=-x^2+O(x^4)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac{e^{2x}-2e^x+1}{\cos3x-2\cos2x+\cos x}=-1+O(x).}
$$
The first nonzero term is the constant $-1$.

