= Solution
{parent=a}

**True.** The <symmetric and antisymmetric parts of a matrix> are, in <Einstein summation convention>,
$$
S_{ij}=\frac{M_{ij}+M_{ji}}2,\qquad A_{ij}=\frac{M_{ij}-M_{ji}}2.
$$
They satisfy $S_{ji}=S_{ij}$, $A_{ji}=-A_{ij}$ and $S_{ij}+A_{ij}=M_{ij}$. This works over the real or complex numbers; it uses division by two.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**False in general.** For symmetric matrices, $(AB)_{ji}=A_{jk}B_{ki}=B_{ik}A_{kj}=(BA)_{ij}$. The <symmetric product criterion for symmetric matrices> is therefore $AB=BA$. For example,
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad B=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad AB=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix}
$$
has $(AB)_{12}=1$ and $(AB)_{21}=0$ despite both factors being symmetric.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**True.** Set $C_{ij}=A_{ki}B_{k\ell}A_{\ell j}$. Exchange $i,j$, interchange the dummy indices $k,\ell$, and use $B_{\ell k}=B_{k\ell}$:
$$
C_{ji}=A_{kj}B_{k\ell}A_{\ell i}=A_{\ell j}B_{\ell k}A_{ki}=A_{ki}B_{k\ell}A_{\ell j}=C_{ij}.
$$
Thus the <matrix congruence> $A^TBA$ preserves symmetry, even when $A$ is singular.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**True.** A <symmetric tensor> and an <antisymmetric tensor> are orthogonal under full index contraction. Renaming the two summed indices gives
$$
A_{ij}B_{ij}=A_{ji}B_{ji}=(-A_{ij})B_{ij}=-A_{ij}B_{ij},
$$
so $\boxed{A_{ij}B_{ij}=0}$. This is a scalar statement with both indices summed, not a claim that every individual entrywise product vanishes.

= e
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

**True.** Orthogonality gives $A_{ki}A_{kj}=\delta_{ij}$ and $A_{ik}A_{jk}=\delta_{ij}$, so $A^T=A^{-1}$. Thus $C_{ij}=A_{ki}B_{k\ell}A_{\ell j}$ is a <similar matrix> to $B$. If $B_{ij}v_j=\lambda v_i$, set $w_i=A_{ji}v_j$. Then
$$
C_{ij}w_j=A_{ki}B_{k\ell}A_{\ell j}A_{mj}v_m=A_{ki}B_{km}v_m=\lambda A_{ki}v_k=\lambda w_i.
$$
The transformed <eigenvector> is nonzero because $A$ is invertible. Applying the inverse transformation gives the reverse correspondence, and <similar matrices> also have the same <characteristic polynomial>, including algebraic multiplicities of all <eigenvalues>.

= 3
