= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $G$ mean that the tested person is guilty and $M$ mean a positive match. Under the specified model, $P(G)=1/N$, $P(M\mid G)=1$ and $P(M\mid G^c)=p$. <Bayes' theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{P(G\mid M)=\frac{(1/N)}{(1/N)+p(1-1/N)}=\frac1{1+(N-1)p}.}
$$
The <posterior probability for a unique source after a positive test> depends on the number of alternative possible sources as well as the false-positive probability. In particular, the false-positive probability $p$ is not the probability of innocence conditional on a positive match.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Under the independent uniform-birthday model, condition on the first person's birthday. Exactly one of the second person's $365$ equally likely dates matches, so
$$
\boxed{P(\text{these two birthdays match})=\frac1{365}.}
$$
This concerns one selected pair; the <birthday problem> allowing any pair among all guests is a different event.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For <matching one specified birthday>, each of the $N-1$ other guests avoids the host's date with probability $364/365$. <Independence> gives
$$
\boxed{P(\text{at least one matches the host})=1-\left(\frac{364}{365}\right)^{N-1}.}
$$
For this to exceed $1/2$, the negative logarithm of $364/365$ reverses the inequality on division, giving $N-1>\ln(1/2)/\ln(364/365)\simeq252.652$. Thus **the smallest total attendance is $N=254$**, including the host.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The quoted pass probabilities on later attempts are conditional on reaching those attempts. If $K$ is the first successful attempt, then
$$
P(K=1)=\frac35,\quad P(K=2)=\frac25\frac45=\frac8{25},\quad P(K=3)=\frac25\frac15\frac78=\frac7{100}.
$$
They sum to $99/100$, so conditioning on success by the third attempt is essential. The <conditional mean of a bounded success time> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[K\mid K\leq3]=\frac{1(3/5)+2(8/25)+3(7/100)}{99/100}=\frac{145}{99}\simeq1.465.}
$$
The remaining probability $1/100$ represents failing all three attempts and is excluded by this conditioning.

