= Solution
{parent=i}

For $t_0\geq0$, integration of the <exponential distribution> gives its <cumulative distribution function>:
$$
\boxed{P(0\leq T\leq t_0)=\int_0^{t_0}\alpha e^{-\alpha t}dt=1-e^{-\alpha t_0}.}
$$
For $t_0<0$ the event is empty and its probability is zero.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <probability density function> is normalized because $\int_0^\infty\alpha e^{-\alpha t}dt=1$. <Integration by parts> gives $\mathbb E[T]=\int_0^\infty t\alpha e^{-\alpha t}dt=1/\alpha$ and $\mathbb E[T^2]=2/\alpha^2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[T]=\frac1\alpha,\qquad\operatorname{Var}(T)=\mathbb E[T^2]-\mathbb E[T]^2=\frac1{\alpha^2}.}
$$
These are the mean and <variance> of the <exponential distribution> with rate $\alpha$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <survival function> of this <exponential distribution> is $P(T>t)=e^{-\alpha t}$. At the <expected value> found above,
$$
\boxed{P(T>\mathbb E[T])=e^{-\alpha(1/\alpha)}=e^{-1}.}
$$

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $s,\tau\geq0$, use <conditional probability> and the <survival function>:
$$
\boxed{P(T>s+\tau\mid T>s)=\frac{e^{-\alpha(s+\tau)}}{e^{-\alpha s}}=e^{-\alpha\tau}.}
$$
It is independent of $s$ and equals $P(T>\tau)$. This is the <memoryless property> of the <exponential distribution>, equivalent here to a constant <hazard function> $\alpha$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Assume $C,Q>0$. The expected net profit per item is $\Pi(\alpha)=Q\mathbb E[T]-C/\alpha^2=Q/\alpha-C/\alpha^2$. Put $u=1/\alpha>0$ and complete the square:
$$
\Pi=Qu-Cu^2=\frac{Q^2}{4C}-C\left(u-\frac Q{2C}\right)^2.
$$
This <quadratic function> has a unique global maximum at $u=Q/(2C)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\alpha_{\rm opt}=\frac{2C}{Q},\qquad\Pi_{\max}=\frac{Q^2}{4C}.}
$$
The calculation uses the expected operating time, rather than replacing lifetime by an arbitrary fixed time.

