= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $\mathbf p=(2,1,1)$, $\mathbf q=(1,2,2)$ and $\mathbf r=(0,0,3)$. <Cross products> give plane normals $\mathbf p\times\mathbf r=(3,-6,0)$ and $\mathbf q\times\mathbf r=(6,-3,0)$. Their dot product is $36$ and each has norm $3\sqrt5$. Thus the acute <angle between two planes> is
$$
\boxed{\theta=\arccos\frac{36}{45}=\arccos\frac45\simeq36.87^\circ.}
$$
This is also the interior dihedral angle here: perpendicular to the common edge $OR$, the interior wedge lies between the projected directions $(2,1)$ and $(1,2)$. The two outward face normals instead make the supplementary angle.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The face $PQR$ has edge vectors $\mathbf q-\mathbf p=(-1,1,1)$ and $\mathbf r-\mathbf p=(-2,-1,2)$. Their <cross product> is $(3,0,3)$, so choose the outward <normal vector> $\mathbf n=(1,0,1)$. The <angle between vectors> obeys
$$
\cos\theta=\frac{\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf n}{|\mathbf p||\mathbf n|}=\frac3{\sqrt6\sqrt2}=\frac{\sqrt3}2,\qquad\boxed{\theta=\frac\pi6=30^\circ.}
$$
Reversing the chosen normal gives the supplementary angle $150^\circ$; the acute angle with the normal line is $30^\circ$.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The magnitude of the <cross product> of two edge vectors is the area of their <parallelogram>. The triangular face occupies half of it:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{area}(PQR)=\frac12|(-1,1,1)\times(-2,-1,2)|=\frac{3\sqrt2}{2}.}
$$

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

With <normal vector> $(1,0,1)$, the plane through $P$ has equation $x+z=3$, also satisfied by $Q,R$. Projecting the displacement from the origin onto the unit normal gives the <distance from a point to a plane>:
$$
\boxed{d=\frac{|3|}{\sqrt{1^2+0^2+1^2}}=\frac3{\sqrt2}.}
$$
The perpendicular foot is $(3/2,0,3/2)$, which lies in that plane and confirms the distance.

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $V\subset\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded region with a piecewise smooth closed boundary $S=\partial V$, and let $\mathbf F$ have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of its closure. The <divergence theorem> states
$$
\boxed{\int_V\nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV=\int_S\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n\,dS,}
$$
where $\mathbf n$ is the outward unit <normal vector>. The right side is the outward <flux>; reversing the orientation reverses its sign.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

On the radius-$a$ sphere, the outward unit <normal vector> is $(x,y,z)/a$, so $\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n=(x^2+2y^2+3z^2)/a$. For a direct <surface integral>, put $x=a\sin\theta\cos\varphi$, $y=a\sin\theta\sin\varphi$, $z=a\cos\theta$ and $dS=a^2\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\varphi$. The integral is
$$
a^3\int_0^{2\pi}\!\int_0^\pi[\sin^2\theta(\cos^2\varphi+2\sin^2\varphi)+3\cos^2\theta]\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\varphi.
$$
The azimuthal integrals are $\pi,\pi,2\pi$, while $\int_0^\pi\sin^3\theta\,d\theta=4/3$ and $\int_0^\pi\cos^2\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta=2/3$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\int_S\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S=a^3\left[3\pi\frac43+6\pi\frac23\right]=8\pi a^3.}
$$
The <flux of a linear field through a sphere> also follows from the equality of the three spherical second moments, without using the <divergence theorem>.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <divergence> is $\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=1+2+3=6$. Since the <volume enclosed by a sphere> is $4\pi a^3/3$,
$$
\boxed{\int_V\nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV=6\frac{4\pi a^3}{3}=8\pi a^3.}
$$
This agrees with the independently computed outward <surface integral>, as the <divergence theorem> requires.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
