= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The logarithm turns the product defining the <factorial> into $\ln(N!)=\sum_{k=2}^N\ln k$. Since $\ln x$ is increasing, each term bounds integrals on its adjacent unit intervals:
$$
\int_{k-1}^k\ln x\,dx\leq\ln k\leq\int_k^{k+1}\ln x\,dx.
$$
Summing from $k=2$ to $N$ proves the <integral bounds for a logarithmic factorial>:
$$
\boxed{N\ln N-N+1\leq\ln(N!)\leq(N+1)\ln(N+1)-N+1-2\ln2.}
$$
For $N=1$, both sides and the empty sum are zero. For $N\geq2$, the rectangle diagram gives the same inequalities geometrically. These bounds establish the leading $N\ln N-N$ growth, while <Stirling's formula> gives finer corrections.

\Image[paper-1-factorial-integral-bounds.png]
{title=Unit rectangles bound the logarithm of a factorial between two integrals}
{description=Original example with $N=6$. Left: each right-endpoint rectangle lies above $\ln x$ on $[k-1,k]$. Right: the same rectangle heights shifted to $[k,k+1]$ lie below $\ln x$. Both rectangle totals equal $\ln(6!)$.}

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $L(N)=(N+a)\ln N-N+b$. The two data points give the linear equations $a\ln10+b=15.104413-10\ln10+10$ and $a\ln50+b=148.477767-50\ln50+50$. Subtraction and back-substitution give
$$
a\simeq0.4958592390,\qquad b\simeq0.9368039780,
$$
or **$a=0.4959$, $b=0.9368$ to four significant figures**. The six-decimal-place value $148.477767$ here is the one printed in the original PDF. Retain guard digits for the estimates below. The fitted coefficients are close to the <Stirling's formula> values $1/2$ and $\tfrac12\ln(2\pi)$ but need not equal them because the fit absorbs finite-$N$ corrections.

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Substituting into the fitted <factorial> logarithm gives
$$
\boxed{\ln(500!)\approx L(500)\simeq2611.3224.}
$$
This is an estimate from the requested two-point fit, not an exact factorial evaluation.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The same fitted <factorial> approximation gives
$$
\boxed{\ln(1000!)\approx L(1000)\simeq5912.1174.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For the stated $N_A=6.022137\times10^{23}$, the leading term $N_A(\ln N_A-1)$ dominates the logarithmic correction $a\ln N_A+b$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\ln(N_A!)\approx L(N_A)\simeq3.237194\times10^{25}.}
$$
This uses the numerical value specified in the question; such a large logarithm can be computed without constructing the <factorial> itself.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Separate the even factors from $1000!$:
$$
1000!=\left(1\cdot3\cdots999\right)\left(2\cdot4\cdots1000\right)=999!!\,2^{500}500!.
$$
The <double factorial> therefore satisfies $\ln(999!!)=\ln(1000!)-500\ln2-\ln(500!)$. Applying the same fit consistently gives
$$
\boxed{\ln(1\cdot3\cdots999)\approx L(1000)-500\ln2-L(500)\simeq2954.2213.}
$$

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
