= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The positive integrand factors into three one-variable functions on a rectangular domain. <Fubini's theorem> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\left(\int_1^2x^{-2}dx\right)\left(\int_1^2y^{-3}dy\right)\left(\int_1^2z^{-4}dz\right)=\frac12\frac38\frac7{24}=\frac7{128}.}
$$

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In first-quadrant <polar coordinates>, $x=r\cos\theta$, $y=r\sin\theta$, and the <Jacobian determinant> contributes $r$. The integrand and area element together become $r^2e^{-r^2}\sin\theta\cos^2\theta\,dr\,d\theta$. Hence
$$
I=\left(\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin\theta\cos^2\theta\,d\theta\right)\left(\int_0^\infty r^2e^{-r^2}dr\right)=\frac13\frac{\sqrt\pi}{4}=\boxed{\frac{\sqrt\pi}{12}}.
$$
The radial integral follows by <integration by parts> from the <Gaussian integral>. The isolated undefined ratio at the origin does not affect this convergent <double integral>.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For fixed $x\ne0$, the <chain rule> and the derivative of the <hyperbolic secant> give
$$
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left[-\frac1x\operatorname{sech}(xy)\right]=\operatorname{sech}(xy)\tanh(xy).
$$
Thus the stated antiderivative holds, with its integration constant independent of $y$ but allowed to depend on $x$. At $x=0$ the integrand is zero and the antiderivative is simply constant; the displayed formula with $1/x$ is meant for nonzero $x$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $x>0$ and $\alpha>1$, the preceding antiderivative gives
$$
\frac{\operatorname{sech}x-\operatorname{sech}(\alpha x)}x=\int_1^\alpha\operatorname{sech}(xy)\tanh(xy)\,dy.
$$
The integrand is nonnegative, so <Tonelli's theorem> permits reversing the two integrals without first assuming absolute integrability. Put $u=xy$ in the inner integral to obtain the <hyperbolic-secant Frullani integral>:
$$
I=\int_1^\alpha\frac{dy}{y}\int_0^\infty\operatorname{sech}u\tanh u\,du=\int_1^\alpha\frac{dy}{y}=\boxed{\ln\alpha}.
$$
Here $\int_0^\infty\operatorname{sech}u\tanh u\,du=[-\operatorname{sech}u]_0^\infty=1$. Near zero, cancellation makes the original integrand $O(x)$; at infinity it decays exponentially. The finite result therefore also establishes ordinary convergence of the <improper integral>.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use <separation of variables> on intervals avoiding zeros of $\cos x$:
$$
\int\frac{dy}{1+y^2}=\int\sec^2x\,dx,\qquad\arctan y=\tan x+C.
$$
Taking the <tangent> gives the real general solution
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\tan(\tan x+C).}
$$
Each solution is restricted to an interval where both the original equation and this expression are finite. There is no real constant solution because $1+y^2$ never vanishes for real $y$.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $x\ne0$, divide by $x$ and use the <integrating factor> $\mu(x)=e^x|x|^\alpha$, separately on each side of zero. The equation becomes $(\mu y)'=|x|^\alpha/x$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y=e^{-x}\left(\frac1\alpha+C|x|^{-\alpha}\right)\quad(\alpha\ne0),\qquad y=e^{-x}(\ln|x|+C)\quad(\alpha=0).}
$$
For $x>0$ the absolute values may be omitted. The original equation is singular as a first-order evolution equation at zero, so these formulas describe regular intervals with $x\ne0$; extension through zero needs a separate regularity condition.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Rearranging gives $y'=2x(y-1)^2$. First retain the <equilibrium> $y=1$. For every other solution, <separation of variables> gives $\int(y-1)^{-2}dy=\int2x\,dx$, so $-1/(y-1)=x^2+C$. Hence
$$
\boxed{y=1,\qquad\text{or}\qquad y=1-\frac1{x^2+C}.}
$$
A nonconstant solution is defined on any interval where its denominator is nonzero. Dividing by $(y-1)^2$ without retaining the equilibrium would lose one solution.

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Factor the differential operator as $(D-a)(D-b)$, where $D=d/dx$. Its homogeneous solutions are $e^{ax},e^{bx}$, while a nonresonant particular solution is $e^{cx}/[(c-a)(c-b)]$. Imposing the two zero initial conditions gives the <exponential forcing response with zero initial data>
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{e^{ax}}{(a-b)(a-c)}+\frac{e^{bx}}{(b-a)(b-c)}+\frac{e^{cx}}{(c-a)(c-b)}.}
$$
One direct derivation avoids a large simultaneous system. Set $v=y'-by$, so $v'-av=e^{cx}$ and $v(0)=0$. The <integrating factor> gives $v=(e^{cx}-e^{ax})/(c-a)$; solving $y'-by=v$ with $y(0)=0$ produces the displayed expression. This also verifies the zero derivative at the origin, since $v(0)=y(0)=0$.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Now $c=b$, so the forcing coincides with a homogeneous exponential and requires a <resonant exponential particular solution>. With $v=y'-by$ as above, $v'-av=e^{bx}$ and $v(0)=0$ give $v=(e^{ax}-e^{bx})/(a-b)$. Therefore
$$
(e^{-bx}y)'=\frac{e^{(a-b)x}-1}{a-b}.
$$
Integrating from zero yields
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{e^{ax}-e^{bx}-(a-b)xe^{bx}}{(a-b)^2},\qquad a\ne b.}
$$
Its numerator starts at order $x^2$, ensuring both initial conditions. It is also the finite coalescing-rate limit $c\to b$ of the nonresonant <exponential forcing response with zero initial data>.

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=11}

Differentiation and factorization give $f'(x)=e^{-x^2}(1+x^2-2x^4)=e^{-x^2}(1-x^2)(1+2x^2)$. The exponential and $1+2x^2$ are positive, so the only <stationary points> are at $x=\pm1$. The derivative is positive for $|x|<1$ and negative for $|x|>1$, giving
$$
\boxed{(-1,-2/e)\text{ a global minimum},\qquad(1,2/e)\text{ a global maximum}.}
$$
The function is <odd>, crosses the origin with slope one, and tends to zero from below as $x\to-\infty$ and from above as $x\to\infty$. These facts determine the sketch.

\Image[paper-1-odd-gaussian-profile.png]
{title=Stationary points and Gaussian decay of the odd polynomial-exponential profile}
{description=Original plot of $(x+x^3)e^{-x^2}$, with its global extrema marked. Shading indicates the equal and opposite signed areas on the two half-lines.}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Since $f$ is <odd> and absolutely integrable, the two signed half-line integrals cancel:
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(x)\,dx=0.}
$$
Absolute integrability follows from Gaussian decay dominating the polynomial factor; no principal-value interpretation is needed.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $u=x^2$, so $x\,dx=du/2$. Then <integration by substitution> gives
$$
\int_0^\infty(x+x^3)e^{-x^2}dx=\frac12\int_0^\infty(1+u)e^{-u}du=\frac12(1+1)=\boxed{1}.
$$
The last two integrals are elementary exponential moments, or values of the <Gamma function>.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <chain rule> gives $ff'=\tfrac12(f^2)'$. Both $f(0)$ and $\lim_{x\to\infty}f(x)$ vanish, so the <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives
$$
\boxed{\int_0^\infty f(x)f'(x)dx=\frac12[f(x)^2]_0^\infty=0.}
$$
The positive contribution while $f$ rises is cancelled by the negative contribution while it falls.

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The periodic extension is an <even> <triangular wave>: peaks of height one occur at the even integers $x=2k$ and valleys of height zero at the odd integers $x=2k+1$. Adjacent peaks and valleys are joined by straight segments of slopes $\pm1$. The function is continuous and has corners at every integer.

\Image[paper-1-triangular-wave.png]
{title=Period-two triangular wave and its first odd cosine harmonics}
{description=Original graph of the periodic extension of $1-|x|$, together with the first one and four nonconstant terms of its Fourier cosine series.}

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Evenness removes every sine coefficient. In the period-two <Fourier cosine series> convention $f=a_0/2+\sum_{n\geq1}a_n\cos(n\pi x)$, the constant coefficient is $a_0=2\int_0^1(1-x)dx=1$. <Integration by parts> gives
$$
a_n=2\int_0^1(1-x)\cos(n\pi x)dx=\frac{2[1-(-1)^n]}{n^2\pi^2}.
$$
Thus the <Fourier cosine series of a triangular wave> is
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac12+\frac4{\pi^2}\sum_{m=0}^\infty\frac{\cos((2m+1)\pi x)}{(2m+1)^2}.}
$$
The periodic function is continuous and piecewise smooth, so the <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem> gives its value at every point. The summable absolute coefficient bound also gives <uniform convergence> by the <Weierstrass M-test>.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Evaluate the <Fourier cosine series of a triangular wave> at its continuous peak $x=0$, where $f(0)=1$. Every cosine is one, so
$$
1=\frac12+\frac4{\pi^2}\sum_{m=0}^\infty\frac1{(2m+1)^2},\qquad\boxed{\sum_{m=0}^\infty\frac1{(2m+1)^2}=\frac{\pi^2}{8}.}
$$
The convergence established above justifies substituting this point into the series.
